**Proposition 6.1 (conditional phase-sensitive extraction).** On the
lift of [UAS] Definition 1.4's canonical acquired domain, suppose a
deterministic initialized fine program actually establishes the
before-image, route and receipt invariants of §§4–5 at every stage,
with constructor identities fixed by actual occurrences and no
unrecorded scheduling choices.
Select the actual retained $M_J$ material of [UAS] Definition 3.3,
together with its actual core phase/snapshot material. If that phase
also uniquely identifies the stage prefix, this joint selection
determines the completed original history, outstanding actual input
and exact fine prefix. The inverse does not give a current actor
permission or a new original $Q$ equality.

Proof. At stable cuts restrict to $M_J$ and apply [UAS] Theorem 3.4
on precisely its canonical acquired domain. At Busy, restore only
the selected old fields and links mathematically and remove only
this identified constructor/receipt prefix. The assumed invariant
gives exactly $M_J(h_c)$ and its old completed meter count. The same
theorem gives $h_c$. The retained admitted site/bit and stage give
the outstanding prefix; deterministic replay of the actual specified
fine program from its initialized entrance gives its previous fine
rows and this partial one. By (OR.1) no accepted input or released
output is silently omitted. Conversely execution of the exact fine
prefix gives its actual selected material. These two directions
yield a conditional fine-history kernel, on this stated domain only.
They use no current-source complement and no executable rollback. ∎

Spatial support remains the actual rooted child-path support; boundary
remains the actual terminal-child antichain; process remains actual
reaction order; retained memory remains a selection of actual backing
and relations. For an implementation preserving a rooted finite child
tree, support and boundary recover each other by prefix closure and
maximality, as already proved in [UAS] Theorem 3.2. Recovering all fine
nonchild incidences additionally requires the stage/head invariant;
tree shape alone does not prove it during a handoff.

On Proposition 2.1's pair the actual controller marker changes,
reaction order gains admission, and selected snapshot material retains
the accepted bit. These natural fine observations distinguish the
pair while the original-predecessor pullback $Q(h_c)$ agrees. Thus
fine-history recovery does not imply the converse kernel direction
from that pullback to any such fine view. It also does not equip
the otherwise undefined original task with an interior Busy response.
No raw/pre-acquisition history inverse follows by replacing actual
variable input by canonical supply.

